S|       1       EXT. ALIA’S COMPOUND - EASTLEIGH - NAIROBI - DAWN        1         
N|               Black. Then the faint glow of hot embers.                          
O|
Y|               ON SCREEN: EASTLEIGH, NAIROBI, KENYA - 06h00                       
O|
N|               A scraping sound as the door of a small brick oven opens.          
O|
N,I|               FATIMA MO’ALLIM, early 30's, slender, Somali, reaches in and       
N|               places loaves of unbaked bread into the wood-fire oven.            
O|
N|               A worn blue headscarf - a hijab - is wrapped around her head       
N|               to cover her hair but still reveals her beautiful face.            
N|               FATIMA runs a small home bakery business within the walls of       
N|               the family compound.                                               
O|
N|               The bakery opens onto a sandy courtyard that surrounds a           
N|               small rough brick home with a corrugated sheet metal roof and      
N|               a separate, small roughly constructed bicycle workshop.            
O|
N|               There are bicycles and bits of bicycles everywhere.                
O|
N,I|               FATIMA’S husband, MUSA (30's) sits on a crate amidst the           
N|               bicycles he repairs putting the finishing touches onto a home      
N|               made “hula hoop” made of black PVC tubing.                         
N,I|               His young daughter, ALIA, 10, watches with impatient               
N|               excitement as he inserts a plastic connector into the tube to      
N|               join the two ends in a hoop.                                       
O|
C,E|                           ALIA                       ALIA (CONT’D)               
U,M|               Is it done, Papa?!             Weli ma dhameyn miyaa Aabe?!        
U,M|                                              (Welli mah dah-main meeyah Ah-      
U,M|                                              be?!)                               
O|
C,E|                           MUSA                       MUSA (CONT’D)               
U,M|               Patience, Alia...              Dulgaado, Alia...                   
U,M|                                              (Dul-gaar-doh, Alia...)             
N|               She fidgets with anticipation.                                     
O|
C,E|                           ALIA                       ALIA (CONT’D)               
U,M|               Do you like the colors I       Maka heshay kalarka aan             
U,M|               used?                          isticmaalayo?                       
U,M|                                              (Maka heh-shay kalarka aan          
U,M|                                              iss-tih-maa-la-yo?)                 
O|
N|               The tubing has been decorated with bright twists of                
N|               electrical tape that form rings of colorful patterns.              
O|
C,E|                           MUSA                       MUSA (CONT’D)               
U,M|               Very pretty...                 Aad iyo Aad La'Jacleey...           
U,M|                                              (Aad iyo aad lah-gel-lay...)        
O|
O|
O|                     YELLOW SHOOTING SCRIPT    12 September 2014    2.            
O|
N|               Scattered around the yard are half a dozen discarded hoops of      
N|               different sizes and colors.                                        
C,E|                           ALIA                       ALIA (CONT’D)               
U,M|               This is my best one ever!      Kani waa midka aan weligeyga        
U,M|                                              Jeelaandoono!                       
U,M|                                              (Kani waa midka aan weli-gay-       
U,M|                                              ist-ti-male-donno!)                 
O|
N|               MUSA smiles, and hands her the hoop. ALIA takes it. She            
N|               swings it over her body and around her waist - and SPINS it.       
O|
N|               She swings her hips, making the hoop swirl around her body.        
C,E|                           ALIA                       ALIA (CONT’D)               
U,M|               Look, Mama!                    Hooyo, Fiiri!                       
U,M|                                              (Hoy-yo Fee-ree!)                   
O|
N|               FATIMA looks out into the yard at her and smiles.                  
O|
N,G|               As ALIA continues to spin the hoop, the CAMERA RISES UP over       
N,G|               her and continues to rise, higher and higher, until we are         
N,G|               seeing:                                                            
O|
N,G|               A BIRD’S-EYE VIEW of the compound within the surrounding           
N|               neighbourhood setting.                                             
N|               Their home is in a Somali Militia controlled neighbourhood of      
N|               Nairobi. The sun has just risen, but people are already on         
N|               the move.                                                          
O|
N|               Beyond the safety and privacy of the walled family compound,       
N|               militia hang out on street corners and guard unofficial            
N|               checkpoint barriers on neighbourhood entrances, making it a        
N|               no-go area for the Kenyan police.                                  
O|
N|               Within this no-go area they stop cars and search anyone they       
N|               suspect of being a Kenyan security police collaborator.            
O|
N|               Four YOUNG MEN drive past Alia’s compound in a pickup truck        
N|               with a machine gun bolted on to the back. They set the             
N|               neighbourhood tone.                                                
N,G|               As we rise higher we reveal that the whole area is a rabbit-       
N|               warren of streets, shops and market stalls.                        
O|
N,T,Y|               We FADE IN our TITLE:                                              
O|
Y|                                   EYE IN THE SKY                                 
O|
N|               Over this we become faintly aware of the low pulse of deep         
N|               bass music...                                                      
T|                                                                CUT TO:           
O|
O|
O|                     YELLOW SHOOTING SCRIPT    12 September 2014    3.            
O|
S|       2       INT. BEDROOM - POWELL’S HOUSE - SURREY - NIGHT           2         
N,I|               COLONEL KATHERINE POWELL lies awake in bed. Her husband SIMON      
N|               sleeps beside her in an eye mask.                                  
O|
Y|               ON SCREEN: SURREY, ENGLAND - 03h15                                 
O|
N|               The thud of music is coming from another room: Quiet, but          
N|               loud enough to be irritating once one becomes aware of it.         
O|
N|               POWELL has become aware of it and it is now irritating her.        
O|
N|               She gets up.                                                       
O|
S|       3       INT. LANDING - POWELL’S HOUSE - SURREY - NIGHT           3         
O|
N|               POWELL, in her pyjamas, exits her bedroom, crosses the             
N|               landing and stops at the bedroom from where the monotonous         
N|               bass is emanating.                                                 
O|
N|               She stands for a moment, wondering whether to enter the room.      
O|
N|               She decides against it.                                            
O|
S|       4       EXT. ALIA’S COMPOUND - EASTLEIGH - DAWN                  4         
N|               ALIA swings her hips as the hoop whizzes around her body.          
O|
S|       5       INT. KITCHEN - POWELL’S HOUSE - SURREY - NIGHT           5         
O|
N|               POWELL, still in her pyjamas but now also in slippers and a        
N|               dressing gown, enters the kitchen.                                 
O|
N|               A Labrador dog, Jesse, gets up out of a basket.                    
O|
N|               POWELL pats the dog’s head, goes to the kitchen tap and            
N|               drinks some water. A routine.                                      
O|
S|       6       EXT. GARDEN - POWELL’S HOUSE - SURREY - NIGHT            6         
O|
N|               POWELL, with Jesse following, exits the house. She walks           
N|               across the garden to an office ‘shed’ and unlocks a bolted         
N|               door. She goes inside.                                             
O|
S|       7       EXT. ALIA’S COMPOUND - EASTLEIGH - DAWN                  7         
O|
N|               ALIA, laughing, swings her hips. The hoop whizzes around her.      
O|
O|
O|                     YELLOW SHOOTING SCRIPT    12 September 2014    4.            
O|
S|       8       INT. POWELL’S GARDEN OFFICE - SURREY - NIGHT             8         
N|               POWELL withdraws a secure dongle from a pocket in her gown         
N|               and plugs it into a USB port in a computer on a desk.              
O|
N|               Jesse knows the routine: he has a basket in here too and he        
N|               lies down in it.                                                   
O|
N|               The office is full of military books, files, mementoes and         
N|               photographs: her entire military career is in this room - and      
N|               we immediately understand that this is where she lives out         
N|               her life. The house is just where she sleeps.                      
O|
N|               While her computer boots up, POWELL glances at a large pin         
N|               board on a wall beside her desk:                                   
N|               It is covered in hand-written notes and SURVEILLANCE               
N|               PHOTOGRAPHS taken in various foreign locations of WANTED           
N|               HVI’s - High Value Individuals.                                    
O|
N|               The faces are mostly Somali, but we also notice a young            
N,I|               British Muslim student named RASHEED HAMUD, and a young            
N,I,M|               American named MUHAMMAD ABDISALAAM. (Note: It does not matter      
N,M|               whether we know their nationality at this point, but their         
N,M|               dress is clearly western.)                                         
O|
N|               One photograph in particular stands out from the rest:             
N,M|               A WHITE WOMAN wearing a deep blue head scarf (a hijab) which       
N|               covers her hair. The name SUSAN HELEN DANFORD appears beneath      
N|               the picture, as well as the name “AYESHA AL-HADY.”                 
O|
N|               Another picture shows Danford without her hijab. She’s an          
N|               attractive woman in her early thirties.                            
O|
N|               In yet another she is with a man named ABDULLAH AL-HADY - who      
N|               we will learn is her husband.                                      
O|
N|               Still other pictures of her show her in various foreign            
N|               locations. A few copies of different PASSPORTS show her image      
N|               with different names: ALLISON WEST, REBECCA SUTTON.                
N|               Below the images of Danford and Al-Hady are dozens of other        
N|               YOUNG FACES - some Western dressed, some local Somali              
N|               fighters with weapons.                                             
O|
N|               Some have been inked out in red.                                   
O|
N|               POWELL stares at the image of Danford for a moment.                
O|
N|               Then her computer BLEEPS and she turns to check her in-box.        
O|
N|               An email message comes up with A PICTURE OF A YOUNG ARMY           
N|               CORPORAL in a wheelchair wearing PROSTHETIC LEGS. We read:         
O|
O|
O|                     YELLOW SHOOTING SCRIPT    12 September 2014    5.            
O|
N|               Dear Colonel Powell                                                
N|               The new legs are good. I can walk to the shops, but I don’t        
N|               see the point in doing that, or in doing anything.                 
O|
N|               Captain Kirby has suggested...                                     
O|
N|               She scrolls to the end:                                            
O|
N|               ... to be honest, I wish I had died.                               
O|
N|               Corporal Shane Allen.                                              
O|
N|               Powell sits staring blankly at the picture of the crippled         
N|               soldier. She is, for a moment, lost. Then she begins to type       
N|               a reply.                                                           
N|               Dear Corporal Allen                                                
O|
N|               Do not lose hope. Once you recover, I promise to assist in         
N|               reassigning you...                                                 
O|
N|               As she types, an INCOMING EMAIL ALERT pops up on her screen.       
O|
N|               Subject: “More news on Ben.”                                       
O|
N|               Powell stops writing her reply to the first email.                 
N|               She hesitates, then opens the new one.                             
O|
N|               We READ:                                                           
O|
N|               This was posted an hour ago.                                       
O|
N|               http://www.al-news.org/watch_now/                                  
O|
N|               Still can’t believe we lost him.                                   
O|
N|               Good luck this morning.                                            
N|               Talk later.                                                        
O|
N|               Frank                                                              
O|
N|               POWELL steels herself. Then reluctantly clicks on the link in      
N|               the email.                                                         
O|
N|               A NEWS VIDEO CLIP opens.                                           
O|
N|               It shows HAND HELD FOOTAGE of A YOUNG, DEAD, AFRICAN MAN in        
N|               plain clothes lying in filth in an alley. He has been shot in      
N|               the back of the head. Blood pools around his shoulders.            
O|
O|
O|                     YELLOW SHOOTING SCRIPT    12 September 2014    6.            
O|
N|               The VOICE of a REPORTER plays over the footage, which cuts to      
N|               various images of Al-Shabaab Militants chanting with weapons       
N|               raised, and graphics of the geographical area described.           
C,E|                                   REPORTER’S VOICE                               
U|                         Somali Al-Shabaab militants have                         
U|                         posted this picture of an unnamed                        
U|                         man they say they have executed in                       
U|                         Nairobi. The group claims he was                         
U|                         working for British military                             
U|                         intelligence attempting to                               
U|                         infiltrate their international                           
U|                         recruitment networks. Al-Shabaab                         
U|                         want to impose their strict version                      
U|                         of Sharia law across the horn of                         
U|                         Africa. They bitterly resent the                         
U|                         role of Britain and the Kenyan                           
U|                         military in propping up the UN                           
U|                         backed Somali government in                              
U|                         Mogadishu. The Ministry of Defense                       
U|                         has declined to comment, but                             
U|                         denounced the execution as                               
U|                         “sickening.”                                             
N|               Affected, but stoic, POWELL watches in icy silence.                
O|
S|       9       EXT. LAS VEGAS - NEVADA - EVENING                        9         
O|
N,G|               FROM HIGH IN THE SKY we drift ominously over suburbs of            
N|               tract homes that pattern the Nevada desert at last light.          
O|
Y|               ON SCREEN:  LAS VEGAS, NEVADA, USA - 20h44                         
O|
S|       10      INT. BEDROOM - STEVE’S HOUSE - LAS VEGAS - NIGHT        10         
O|
N|               Dark. A large digital alarm clock reads: 20h44                     
O|
N|               A sleeping body is dimly visible with covers over its head.        
N|               The clock clicks to 20h45 and the radio comes on: Indie rock.      
O|
N,I|               STEVE WATTS, 25, reluctantly moves the covers aside and flips      
N|               on a side lamp.                                                    
O|
N|               He lies staring at the ceiling for a moment, listening to his      
N|               music, still half asleep, not wanting to get up just yet.          
O|
N|               The room is sparsely decorated. On his bedroom wall is a           
N|               single, large framed aviation art painting by Randy Green of       
N|               “The Boys from Richmond” fighter jets against a striking sky.      
O|
O|
O|                     YELLOW SHOOTING SCRIPT    12 September 2014    7.            
O|
N|               On a small desk is a framed picture of Steve graduating from       
N|               University in Nevada, a plaque from a Reserve Officer              
N|               Training Corps showing him commissioned as a 2nd Lieutenant,       
N,M|               and an Undergraduate RPA (Remotely Piloted Aircraft) training      
N|               certificate. Finally there is a photo of Steve himself posing      
N|               in a flight suit next to a training aeroplane.                     
O|
S|       11      EXT. COUNTRYSIDE - SURREY - DAWN                        11         
O|
N,G|               We drift over beautiful English countryside as dawn breaks.        
O|
Y|               ON SCREEN:  SURREY, ENGLAND                                        
O|
S|       12      EXT. COUNTRY LANE - SURREY - DAWN                       12         
O|
N|               POWELL, now in Wellington boots and a weatherproof jacket,         
N|               walks with Jesse. There is a weariness about her. A profound       
N|               sadness lurking just beneath her tough exterior.                   
O|
N|               She tosses a stick for her dog. There’s an edge of                 
N|               aggression, of unexpressed anger in the throw.                     
O|
N|               Jesse chases it, happy.                                            
O|
